                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I literally remember years ago opening this game wanting to play it with a controller because it feels like a controller game but it just wanted to spin me right 'round baby so I closed it and didn't think about it until recently when I stared at it and I was like... I wonder... But I opened it up and the camera just spun and spun and spun until I hurled so I did the sensible thing and stopped trying to play merry-go-round hard mode.\n\nFine. Keyboard and mouse it is. Ugh.\n\nAnyway, this is supposed to be a Souls-like and I guess it is? I personally see the idea of it but not so much the implementation. This game felt mechanically slow and deliberate but most bosses are cheese-able either because you can just run in a fucking circle til they do an attack and you have a window of opportunity or you can use the environment to get them stuck and just wail on them til kingdom come. If that makes a Souls game then I sit corrected but from what I've seen of those games, they seem to have a few different ways to play them like precision based methodology or fucking around with build strategies to try and mitigate the super punishing harshness bosses can yeet onto you. In this game, maybe you find a shred of armour but for the most part, you have your dinky weapon and a torch you can't even burn enemies with and your gameplay revolves around that. Oh and little candles of hope scattered around doing heavy lifting to give you just a measly bit of health left for a second wind when you're in battle, which reeks of dark humour somehow.\n\nThe story feels like a cop out not because it's bad but because corners had to be cut. So the cryptic-ness felt like backpedaling into safer territory rather than purposeful meaning making. We see some political intrigue in the storyline, the Ars Goetia referenced through the character designs as well as Dante's Inferno set dressed through words and in universe understanding. For instance, our protagonist finds himself deep in hell, in a palace called Malebolgia, with a torch evoking \"Virgil\" and a halberd. Like... The references are there. As for the narrative, at least my interpretation of it because I am a generous and forgiving interpreter, is about coming to terms with your past, the choices you made, the demons that haunt you that you have built up so much because you are riddled with guilt and shame that you can't remember the core, just the idea of intention and you think, [i]yeah I totally can overcome this[/i] only to realize you can't and become part of the palace you are trapped in, part of the horde. Was the hell real or was the hell what you created it to be? *cue Twilight Zone theme*\n\nHonestly the whole shebang wasn't that bad. It's basically a walking simulator with some basic but tilted combat. The game understands the infernal atmosphere and visual language more than systemic design and that's ok. Sometimes we explore stuff for the fuck of it and come out with an understanding that experiences aren't for everyone but everyone will experience something. Now if you'll excuse me, I have to see a demon about a soul.",
